], "source": "labyrinth"}, {"id": "p.U.4", "title": "unique partner: |det K_{B-s}| = 1", "statement": "Zeroing the entries of s in K_B gives a determinant equal to the number of partners up to sign. A tree matching is a state where it is 1.", "where": ["research/2026-11-14_sol_machinery-literature/REFORMULATIONS.md 2 (after T Prop. 2.1)"], "tags": ["step U"], "created": "2026-11-14", "updated": "2026-11-14", "kind": "theorem", "tier": "T2", "status": "established: proved here (unreviewed)", "review": {"state": "unreviewed", "date": "2026-11-14"}, "links": [{"to": "m.step.U", "rel": "instance-of"}, {"to": "src.agent.mach", "rel": "cites"}, {"to": "wp.FP", "rel": "supports"}], "source": "labyrinth"}, {"id": "p.U.5", "title": "tree matchings = partnered Hamiltonian cycles", "statement": "Tree matchings are in bijection with the Hamiltonian cycles avoiding e0* whose state has a partner. So 3 #TM <= #HC(G).", "where": ["research/2026-11-14_fable_route-combinatorics/PROOF.md Prop. 7; research/2026-11-14_astra_limits/HYPOTHESES.md; research/2026-11-14_opus_route-graph-theory/PROOFS.md Thm 7.2 (explicit left inverse; image = partitions with acyclic white parent map)"], "tags": ["step U"], "created": "2026-11-14", "updated": "2026-11-14", "kind": "theorem", "tier": "T2", "status": "established: proved here (1 independent AI referee; human check pending)", "review": {"state": "refereed", "date": "2026-11-14", "by": ["research/2026-11-14_astra_referee-combinatorics"], "verdict": "proof in research/2026-11-14_fable_route-combinatorics: FOLLOWS, with corrections to domain, normalisation and the scope of the negative conclusions (REFEREE.md); human check pending. Not covered by that verdict: 2026-11-14_astra_limits, 2026-11-14_opus_route-graph-theory"}, "links": [{"to": "m.step.U", "rel": "instance-of"}, {"to": "src.agent.comb", "rel": "cites"}, {"to": "src.agent.lim", "rel": "cites"}, {"to": "src.agent.gt", "rel": "cites"}, {"to": "wp.FP", "rel": "supports"}, {"to": "x.A.x1", "rel": "refutes"}], "source": "labyrinth"}, {"id": "p.U.6", "title": "rooted functional orientations; two-sided hypertrees", "statement": "Two more exact restatements of the target, as an orientation problem and as a simultaneous hypertree realisation.", "where": ["research/2026-11-14_sol_machinery-literature/REFORMULATIONS.md 5, 7"], "tags": ["step U"], "created": "2026-11-14", "updated": "2026-11-14", "kind": "theorem", "tier": "T2", "status": "established: proved here (unreviewed)", "review": {"state": "unreviewed", "date": "2026-11-14"}, "links": [{"to": "m.step.U", "rel": "instance-of"}, {"to": "src.agent.mach", "rel": "cites"}, {"to": "wp.FP", "rel": "supports"}], "source": "labyrinth"}, {"id": "x.U.x1", "title": "matching matroid meets graphic matroid", "statement": "Treat the two endpoint constraints as one matroid and use Edmonds' two-matroid intersection.", "where": ["research/2026-11-14_sol_machinery-literature/REFORMULATIONS.md 6; research/2026-11-14_sol_route-constructive/data/other_candidates.json; research/2026-11-14_fable_referee-determinants/REFEREE-constructive.md"], "tags": ["step U"], "created": "2026-11-14", "updated": "2026-11-14", "kind": "deadend", "tier": null, "status": "refuted", "lesson": "Matchings are not a matroid (augmentation fails on K_{2,2}). With only the face partition and the white graphic matroid, the augmenting-path algorithm returns white trees that repeat vertices.", "note": "Smallest known counterexample: Hexagonal prism: 4 of the 6 common bases of the two-matroid relaxation are not states (referee's correction, refd). Larger witness: truncated octahedron, eleven spokes repeating non-roots 3 and 4 (con/data/other_candidates.json).", "links": [{"to": "m.step.U", "rel": "instance-of"}, {"to": "src.agent.mach", "rel": "cites"}, {"to": "src.agent.con", "rel": "cites"}, {"to": "src.agent.refd", "rel": "cites"}], "source": "labyrinth"}, {"id": "x.U.x2", "title": "unconstrained white spanning trees", "statement": "Every spanning tree of the white-face graph, or of its restriction to duals of B, gives a tree matching.", "where": ["research/2026-11-14_astra_route-dimers/ROUTES.md; research/2026-11-14_sol_route-constructive/ALGORITHMS.md (wilson_candidate)"], "tags": ["step U"], "created": "2026-11-14", "updated": "2026-11-14", "kind": "deadend", "tier": null, "status": "refuted", "lesson": "Both label constraints are needed. Rejection sampling accepted 0 of 100 trees on the larger named graphs.", "note": "Smallest known counterexample: Octahedron: 128 white trees, 8 after restricting to duals of B, 2 tree matchings.", "links": [{"to": "m.step.U", "rel": "instance-of"}, {"to": "src.agent.dim", "rel": "cites"}, {"to": "src.agent.con", "rel": "cites"}], "source": "labyrinth"}, {"id": "x.U.x3", "title": "trinity arborescences = tree matchings", "statement": "The Hine-Kalman bijection carries all states to arborescences; perhaps every arborescence is a tree matching.", "where": ["research/2026-11-14_astra_route-dimers/ROUTES.md (trees and trinity)"], "tags": ["step U"], "created": "2026-11-14", "updated": "2026-11-14", "kind": "deadend", "tier": null, "status": "refuted", "lesson": "Arborescences count all states. Tree matchings are a proper subset with no known one-colour criterion.", "note": "Smallest known counterexample: Hexagonal prism dual: 3 arborescences in each colour, 2 tree matchings.", "links": [{"to": "m.step.U", "rel": "instance-of"}, {"to": "src.agent.dim", "rel": "cites"}], "source": "labyrinth"}, {"id": "x.U.x4", "title": "Yutsis partition of G", "statement": "Identify the two induced trees with a Yutsis decomposition of the cubic graph.", "where": ["research/2026-11-14_sol_machinery-literature/REFORMULATIONS.md 11"], "tags": ["step U"], "created": "2026-11-14", "updated": "2026-11-14", "kind": "deadend", "tier": null, "status": "refuted", "lesson": "Different ground sets: the vertices of G against the vertices of T.", "note": "Smallest known counterexample: definitional", "links": [{"to": "m.step.U", "rel": "instance-of"}, {"to": "src.agent.mach", "rel": "cites"}], "source": "labyrinth"}, {"id": "h.U.1h", "title": "support of the tree-degree polynomial", "statement": "Show the target degree vector d* lies in the support of det L_H(y), from plane incidence inequalities.", "where": ["research/2026-11-14_sol_machinery-literature/ROUTE-IDEAS.md 2; research/2026-11-14_opus_route-topology/PREDICTIONS.md P8, P9"], "tags": ["step U"], "created": "2026-11-14", "updated": "2026-11-14", "kind": "hunch", "tier": "T6", "status": "live", "vivid": "An unsigned target: no cancellation to explain. If every lattice point of the degree polytope were realised (P9), convexity would finish.", "test": "Enumerate H-trees on the cube and the hexagonal bipyramid; compare the support with the lattice points of its convex hull.", "links": [{"to": "m.step.U", "rel": "instance-of"}, {"to": "src.agent.mach", "rel": "cites"}, {"to": "src.agent.topo", "rel": "cites"}, {"to": "wp.FP", "rel": "suggests"}], "source": "labyrinth"}, {"id": "h.U.2h", "title": "spanning-tree parity / matroid parity", "statement": "'Each black centre keeps exactly two of its three spokes' looks like the spanning tree parity problem that Feder and Subi solve in polynomial time for 2- and 3-sided faces.", "where": ["research/2026-11-14_opus_history-literature/REPORT.md (leads); research/2026-11-14_sol_machinery-literature/ROUTE-IDEAS.md 10"], "tags": ["step U"], "created": "2026-11-14", "updated": "2026-11-14", "kind": "hunch", "tier": "T6", "status": "live", "vivid": "If it is a matroid parity instance, Lovasz gives a polynomial algorithm, against 3^(N/4).", "test": "Design the gadget for one black centre and two adjacent centres; enumerate feasible parity solutions against partial spoke choices.", "links": [{"to": "m.step.U", "rel": "instance-of"}, {"to": "src.agent.hist", "rel": "cites"}, {"to": "src.agent.mach", "rel": "cites"}, {"to": "wp.FP", "rel": "suggests"}], "source": "labyrinth"}, {"id": "h.U.4h", "title": "Bernardi process, Schnyder woods, blossom trees", "statement": "A canonical tree or orientation of the map might produce a tree matching directly.", "where": ["research/2026-11-14_sol_machinery-literature/ROUTE-IDEAS.md 4, 11, 12, 13"], "tags": ["step U"], "created": "2026-11-14", "updated": "2026-11-14", "kind": "hunch", "tier": "T6", "status": "live", "vivid": "Each theory uses three colours and rooted trees on triangulations, as the target does.", "test": "On the octahedron and the hexagonal bipyramid, test every facewise corner rule for bijectivity on X and for the forest condition.", "links": [{"to": "m.step.U", "rel": "instance-of"}, {"to": "src.agent.mach", "rel": "cites"}, {"to": "wp.FP", "rel": "suggests"}], "source": "labyrinth"}, {"id": "k.U.5h", "title": "order-theoretic position in the state lattice", "statement": "Outcome (topo): the tree matchings are neither an interval nor an antichain of the clock lattice, and the top and bottom states are often tree matchings. No order-theoretic pattern was found.", "where": ["research/2026-11-14_opus_route-topology/PREDICTIONS.md P6; research/2026-11-14_sol_machinery-literature/REFORMULATIONS.md 9"], "tags": ["step U"], "created": "2026-11-14", "updated": "2026-11-14", "kind": "conjecture", "tier": "T5", "status": "open", "test": "Decided on the topology agent's sample (166 lattices); a description of the subset is still missing.", "links": [{"to": "m.step.U", "rel": "instance-of"}, {"to": "src.agent.topo", "rel": "cites"}, {"to": "src.agent.mach", "rel": "cites"}, {"to": "wp.FP", "rel": "suggests"}], "source": "labyrinth"}, {"id": "k.U.6h", "title": "describe partnered Hamiltonian cycles in G alone", "statement": "There is a description, without states, of the Hamiltonian cycles of G avoiding e0* whose state has a partner.", "where": ["research/2026-11-14_fable_route-combinatorics/REPORT.md, open question 3"], "tags": ["step U"], "created": "2026-11-14", "updated": "2026-11-14", "kind": "conjecture", "tier": "T5", "status": "open", "test": "For the hexagonal prism list the 2 partnered and 2 unpartnered avoiding cycles and compare their face sequences; then test any candidate rule on the 176726 unpartnered cycles of the survey.", "links": [{"to": "m.step.U", "rel": "instance-of"}, {"to": "src.agent.comb", "rel": "cites"}, {"to": "wp.FP", "rel": "suggests"}], "source": "labyrinth"}, {"id": "th.R.1", "title": "out-degree count", "statement": "A tree component with v vertices and a roots has v - a out-edges and v - 1 edges, so a = 1. T: s is a tree matching iff it has exactly one partner.", "where": ["O Lemma 6.1; L Lemma 8; T (3), Prop. 2.1, Thm 1.2"], "tags": ["step R"], "created": "2026-11-14", "updated": "2026-11-14", "kind": "theorem", "tier": "T1", "status": "established (LIT: 2026 preprint or its formalisation, unrefereed)", "links": [{"to": "m.step.R", "rel": "instance-of"}, {"to": "src.O", "rel": "cites"}, {"to": "src.L", "rel": "cites"}, {"to": "src.T", "rel": "cites"}, {"to": "wp.IT", "rel": "supports"}], "source": "labyrinth"}, {"id": "c.R.c", "title": "unique partner checked on 140358 states", "statement": "Brute force over all states: tree matching iff exactly one partner; the partner count is 2^k or 0.", "where": ["research/2026-11-14_fable_route-combinatorics/lab/extra2.py 16; research/2026-11-14_sol_route-constructive/VERIFICATION.md"], "tags": ["step R"], "created": "2026-11-14", "updated": "2026-11-14", "kind": "evidence", "tier": "T4", "status": "computed", "evidence": ["research/2026-11-14_fable_route-combinatorics/lab/extra2.py 16; research/2026-11-14_sol_route-constructive/VERIFICATION.md"], "links": [{"to": "m.step.R", "rel": "instance-of"}, {"to": "src.agent.comb", "rel": "cites"}, {"to": "src.agent.con", "rel": "cites"}, {"to": "wp.IT", "rel": "tests"}], "source": "labyrinth"}, {"id": "x.R.x1", "title": "a forest state is enough", "statement": "Call s good as soon as Q_s is acyclic, with no partner assumed.", "where": ["research/2026-11-14_astra_limits/HYPOTHESES.md (forest versus rooted forest); research/2026-11-14_fable_dependency-graph/correspondence.md 3.5"], "tags": ["step R"], "created": "2026-11-14", "updated": "2026-11-14", "kind": "deadend", "tier": null, "status": "refuted", "lesson": "Without the partner the out-degree count is unavailable: a component can hold two roots and another none. 'Forest', 'cycle-breaking' and 'tree matching' agree for pairs and differ for single states.", "note": "Smallest known counterexample: Hexagonal prism dual: all 3 states have acyclic Q_s, only 2 are tree matchings.", "links": [{"to": "m.step.R", "rel": "instance-of"}, {"to": "src.agent.lim", "rel": "cites"}, {"to": "src.agent.dep", "rel": "cites"}], "source": "labyrinth"}, {"id": "th.E.1", "title": "tree-cotree duality", "statement": "The duals of the deleted spokes form a spanning tree on the white faces.", "where": ["O Lemma 2.1; L Cor. 27; T 7.1"], "tags": ["step E"], "created": "2026-11-14", "updated": "2026-11-14", "kind": "theorem", "tier": "T1", "status": "established (LIT: 2026 preprint or its formalisation, unrefereed)", "links": [{"to": "m.step.E", "rel": "instance-of"}, {"to": "src.O", "rel": "cites"}, {"to": "src.L", "rel": "cites"}, {"to": "src.T", "rel": "cites"}, {"to": "wp.WT", "rel": "supports"}], "source": "labyrinth"}, {"id": "h.E.2", "title": "Temperley / Kenyon-Propp-Wilson", "statement": "Read the step as the tree-to-matching bijection of KPW.", "where": ["research/2026-11-14_fable_dependency-graph/skeleton.md E2; research/2026-11-14_astra_route-dimers/ROUTES.md"], "tags": ["step E"], "created": "2026-11-14", "updated": "2026-11-14", "kind": "hunch", "tier": "T6", "status": "live", "vivid": "KPW applied to the white graph encodes all its spanning trees and carries the label constraints along; it does not identify those dimers with B (dim).", "test": "Write the bijection for the octahedron and the n = 15 example.", "links": [{"to": "m.step.E", "rel": "instance-of"}, {"to": "src.agent.dep", "rel": "cites"}, {"to": "src.agent.dim", "rel": "cites"}, {"to": "wp.WT", "rel": "suggests"}], "source": "labyrinth"}, {"id": "th.E2.1", "title": "orient towards y0", "statement": "Each tree edge is assigned to its end farther from y0 and selects the side of its black face bordering that end.", "where": ["O 6.1; L Claim 31; T 7.1"], "tags": ["step E2"], "created": "2026-11-14", "updated": "2026-11-14", "kind": "theorem", "tier": "T1", "status": "established (LIT: 2026 preprint or its formalisation, unrefereed)", "links": [{"to": "m.step.E2", "rel": "instance-of"}, {"to": "src.O", "rel": "cites"}, {"to": "src.L", "rel": "cites"}, {"to": "src.T", "rel": "cites"}, {"to": "wp.SE", "rel": "supports"}], "source": "labyrinth"}, {"id": "p.E2.2", "title": "the three papers select the same P", "statement": "Li's trace of the dual tree through black vertices gives the same set of edges of G.", "where": ["research/2026-11-14_fable_dependency-graph/correspondence.md 3.6"], "tags": ["step E2"], "created": "2026-11-14", "updated": "2026-11-14", "kind": "theorem", "tier": "T2", "status": "established: proved here (unreviewed)", "review": {"state": "unreviewed", "date": "2026-11-14"}, "links": [{"to": "m.step.E2", "rel": "instance-of"}, {"to": "src.agent.dep", "rel": "cites"}, {"to": "wp.SE", "rel": "supports"}], "source": "labyrinth"}, {"id": "th.F.OT", "title": "P is a forest, by the disk identity", "statement": "A cycle in P0 would be directed, and the selected-side count gives a disk with one black face fewer than the state requires. Then duality gives the cycle and the Jordan curve the two induced trees.", "where": ["O Lemma 6.2; T Lemma 7.1"], "tags": ["step F"], "created": "2026-11-14", "updated": "2026-11-14", "kind": "theorem", "tier": "T1", "status": "established (LIT: 2026 preprint or its formalisation, unrefereed)", "links": [{"to": "m.step.F", "rel": "instance-of"}, {"to": "src.O", "rel": "cites"}, {"to": "src.T", "rel": "cites"}, {"to": "wp.JC", "rel": "supports"}], "source": "labyrinth"}, {"id": "th.F.L", "title": "innermost cycle, no disk identity", "statement": "If the complement had two cycles, an innermost one with no root face inside bounds a tree of faces, each with an out-step; some step is undone and two faces sit on one black vertex.", "where": ["L Lemma 30"], "tags": ["step F"], "created": "2026-11-14", "updated": "2026-11-14", "kind": "theorem", "tier": "T1", "status": "established (LIT: 2026 preprint or its formalisation, unrefereed)", "links": [{"to": "m.step.F", "rel": "instance-of"}, {"to": "src.L", "rel": "cites"}, {"to": "wp.JC", "rel": "supports"}], "source": "labyrinth"}, {"id": "p.F.1", "title": "W - B = 1 against W - B = 3", "statement": "The disk-identity argument restated as two incompatible counts of white minus black faces.", "where": ["research/2026-11-14_fable_route-combinatorics/PROOF.md Section 5"], "tags": ["step F"], "created": "2026-11-14", "updated": "2026-11-14", "kind": "theorem", "tier": "T2", "status": "established: proved here (1 independent AI referee; human check pending)", "review": {"state": "refereed", "date": "2026-11-14", "by": ["research/2026-11-14_astra_referee-combinatorics"], "verdict": "proof in research/2026-11-14_fable_route-combinatorics: FOLLOWS, with corrections to domain, normalisation and the scope of the negative conclusions (REFEREE.md); human check pending"}, "links": [{"to": "m.step.F", "rel": "instance-of"}, {"to": "src.agent.comb", "rel": "cites"}, {"to": "wp.JC", "rel": "supports"}, {"to": "th.F.OT", "rel": "modifies"}], "source": "labyrinth"}, {"id": "p.F.2", "title": "forest certificate implies Hamiltonian cycle", "statement": "Conditional conversion: a spanning forest selecting one side per face has a complementary dual that is a Hamiltonian cycle.", "where": ["research/2026-11-14_astra_exposition-method/DEPENDENCY-AUDIT.md"], "tags": ["step F"], "created": "2026-11-14", "updated": "2026-11-14", "kind": "theorem", "tier": "T2", "status": "established: proved here (unreviewed)", "review": {"state": "unreviewed", "date": "2026-11-14"}, "links": [{"to": "m.step.F", "rel": "instance-of"}, {"to": "src.agent.expo", "rel": "cites"}, {"to": "wp.JC", "rel": "supports"}], "source": "labyrinth"}, {"id": "p.F.3", "title": "recovery is injective, with known image", "statement": "For fixed t0 and e0 the map from tree matchings to cycles is injective. Its image is the set of avoiding cycles whose state is a tree matching.", "where": ["research/2026-11-14_astra_limits/HYPOTHESES.md; research/2026-11-14_fable_route-combinatorics/PROOF.md Section 6"], "tags": ["step F"], "created": "2026-11-14", "updated": "2026-11-14", "kind": "theorem", "tier": "T2", "status": "established: proved here (1 independent AI referee; human check pending)", "review": {"state": "refereed", "date": "2026-11-14", "by": ["research/2026-11-14_astra_referee-combinatorics"], "verdict": "proof in research/2026-11-14_fable_route-combinatorics: FOLLOWS, with corrections to domain, normalisation and the scope of the negative conclusions (REFEREE.md); human check pending. Not covered by that verdict: 2026-11-14_astra_limits"}, "links": [{"to": "m.step.F", "rel": "instance-of"}, {"to": "src.agent.lim", "rel": "cites"}, {"to": "src.agent.comb", "rel": "cites"}, {"to": "wp.JC", "rel": "supports"}], "source": "labyrinth"}, {"id": "c.F.c", "title": "recovery checked on every tree matching", "statement": "Every selected P is a two-component forest and both sides induce trees.", "where": ["research/2026-11-14_sol_route-constructive/VERIFICATION.md (744 recoveries); research/2026-11-14_astra_limits/code/campaign.py 14 (3618 certificates)"], "tags": ["step F"], "created": "2026-11-14", "updated": "2026-11-14", "kind": "evidence", "tier": "T4", "status": "computed", "evidence": ["research/2026-11-14_sol_route-constructive/VERIFICATION.md (744 recoveries); research/2026-11-14_astra_limits/code/campaign.py 14 (3618 certificates)"], "links": [{"to": "m.step.F", "rel": "instance-of"}, {"to": "src.agent.con", "rel": "cites"}, {"to": "src.agent.lim", "rel": "cites"}, {"to": "wp.JC", "rel": "tests"}], "source": "labyrinth"}, {"id": "k.F.4", "title": "remove a leaf of the white tree", "statement": "Removing a leaf of the white tree with the black face whose spoke it crosses leaves a rooted triangulation with a tree matching, and the cycle of the larger graph is a local detour.", "where": ["research/2026-11-14_fable_dependency-graph/skeleton.md F1"], "tags": ["step F"], "created": "2026-11-14", "updated": "2026-11-14", "kind": "conjecture", "tier": "T5", "status": "open", "test": "Implement leaf removal on the trees produced by dep/scripts/check_nodes.py and check validity of the smaller object.", "links": [{"to": "m.step.F", "rel": "instance-of"}, {"to": "src.agent.dep", "rel": "cites"}, {"to": "wp.JC", "rel": "suggests"}], "source": "labyrinth"}, {"id": "h.F.5", "title": "a potential along the selected out-edges", "statement": "Follow selected out-edges from a vertex to a root; a quantity that decreases along the walk proves connectivity.", "where": ["research/2026-11-14_fable_dependency-graph/skeleton.md F2"], "tags": ["step F"], "created": "2026-11-14", "updated": "2026-11-14", "kind": "hunch", "tier": "T6", "status": "live", "vivid": "A third proof of the last step, perhaps the shortest.", "test": "Compute the change of white-tree depth along each selected out-edge.", "links": [{"to": "m.step.F", "rel": "instance-of"}, {"to": "src.agent.dep", "rel": "cites"}, {"to": "wp.JC", "rel": "suggests"}], "source": "labyrinth"}, {"id": "th.J2.1", "title": "Jordan curve theorem and Euler count", "statement": "Each side of the cycle induces a connected acyclic subgraph of T.", "where": ["O end of 6.2; T Lemma 7.1; APSW Lemma 5"], "tags": ["step J2"], "created": "2026-11-14", "updated": "2026-11-14", "kind": "theorem", "tier": "T1", "status": "established (LIT: published)", "links": [{"to": "m.step.J2", "rel": "instance-of"}, {"to": "src.O", "rel": "cites"}, {"to": "src.T", "rel": "cites"}, {"to": "wp.2T", "rel": "supports"}], "source": "labyrinth"}, {"id": "th.J2.2", "title": "Li does not need the trees", "statement": "Li goes from P to the Hamiltonian cycle and glues cycles.", "where": ["L Section 5"], "tags": ["step J2"], "created": "2026-11-14", "updated": "2026-11-14", "kind": "theorem", "tier": "T1", "status": "established (LIT: 2026 preprint or its formalisation, unrefereed)", "links": [{"to": "m.step.J2", "rel": "instance-of"}, {"to": "src.L", "rel": "cites"}, {"to": "wp.2T", "rel": "supports"}], "source": "labyrinth"}, {"id": "th.G.O", "title": "induction with a prescribed cap edge", "statement": "Cut at a separating triangle, cap, solve one side, prescribe the same-label edge on the other, glue. O inducts on Theorem 2.2, L takes a minimum counterexample.", "where": ["O Section 7; L Appendix C"], "tags": ["step G"], "created": "2026-11-14", "updated": "2026-11-14", "kind": "theorem", "tier": "T1", "status": "established (LIT: 2026 preprint or its formalisation, unrefereed)", "links": [{"to": "m.step.G", "rel": "instance-of"}, {"to": "src.O", "rel": "cites"}, {"to": "src.L", "rel": "cites"}, {"to": "wp.GL", "rel": "supports"}], "source": "labyrinth"}, {"id": "th.G.T", "title": "cut only at black-inner triangles", "statement": "The exact criterion says when a cut is needed.", "where": ["T 7.3"], "tags": ["step G"], "created": "2026-11-14", "updated": "2026-11-14", "kind": "theorem", "tier": "T1", "status": "established (LIT: 2026 preprint or its formalisation, unrefereed)", "links": [{"to": "m.step.G", "rel": "instance-of"}, {"to": "src.T", "rel": "cites"}, {"to": "wp.GL", "rel": "supports"}], "source": "labyrinth"}, {"id": "th.G.K", "title": "Kelmans' reduction (1986)", "statement": "The reduction to cyclically 4-edge-connected graphs with an (x+ y+) or avoided-edge hypothesis was known. O re-proves it without citing it for this step.", "where": ["Kelmans 1986/1994 via APSW 2016 Thm 8; Hertel 2005"], "tags": ["step G"], "created": "2026-11-14", "updated": "2026-11-14", "kind": "theorem", "tier": "T1", "status": "established (LIT: published)", "links": [{"to": "m.step.G", "rel": "instance-of"}, {"to": "wp.GL", "rel": "supports"}], "source": "labyrinth"}, {"id": "p.G.1", "title": "every separating triangle has one black side", "statement": "The state method needs no cut exactly when t0 is on the black side of every separating triangle. Changing the root or swapping colours can avoid cuts, not always.", "where": ["research/2026-11-14_fable_dependency-graph/audit-trees.md T-F1; research/2026-11-14_fable_dependency-graph/skeleton.md G1"], "tags": ["step G"], "created": "2026-11-14", "updated": "2026-11-14", "kind": "theorem", "tier": "T2", "status": "established: proved here (unreviewed)", "review": {"state": "unreviewed", "date": "2026-11-14"}, "links": [{"to": "m.step.G", "rel": "instance-of"}, {"to": "src.agent.dep", "rel": "cites"}, {"to": "wp.GL", "rel": "supports"}], "source": "labyrinth"}, {"id": "p.G.2", "title": "the gluing needs the same-label edge", "statement": "Trees of the two pieces meet in one vertex or one edge; prescribing the cap edge supplies the agreement. The smallest separating example has 9 vertices.", "where": ["research/2026-11-14_astra_limits/HYPOTHESES.md (separating triangles)"], "tags": ["step G"], "created": "2026-11-14", "updated": "2026-11-14", "kind": "theorem", "tier": "T2", "status": "established: proved here (under review)", "review": {"state": "under-review", "date": "2026-11-14", "by": ["research/2026-11-14_sol_referee-structure"], "verdict": "proof in research/2026-11-14_astra_limits: pending (audit started 10:50 UTC)"}, "links": [{"to": "m.step.G", "rel": "instance-of"}, {"to": "src.agent.lim", "rel": "cites"}, {"to": "wp.GL", "rel": "supports"}], "source": "labyrinth"}, {"id": "x.G.x1", "title": "prescribe whatever each cut needs", "statement": "Strengthen the induction hypothesis to an arbitrary set of prescribed edges.", "where": ["Bagheri Gh. et al. 2022, proof of Thm 4.6; research/2026-11-14_opus_history-literature/WHAT-WAS-HARD.md 6"], "tags": ["step G"], "created": "2026-11-14", "updated": "2026-11-14", "kind": "deadend", "tier": null, "status": "refuted", "lesson": "Hamiltonicity through an arbitrary prescribed edge set is NP-complete for Barnette graphs. The hypothesis must prescribe something bounded: one avoided edge.", "note": "Smallest known counterexample: reduction from Akiyama-Nishizeki-Saito instances", "links": [{"to": "m.step.G", "rel": "instance-of"}], "source": "labyrinth"}, {"id": "k.G.3", "title": "a cut-free root", "statement": "Count how often some root (black face on the black side of all separating triangles, or the same with colours swapped) exists.", "where": ["research/2026-11-14_fable_dependency-graph/skeleton.md G1"], "tags": ["step G"], "created": "2026-11-14", "updated": "2026-11-14", "kind": "conjecture", "tier": "T5", "status": "open", "test": "Over generated Barnette graphs with several separating triangles, compute the fraction with a cut-free root; three triangles with alternating black sides should give the first failure.", "links": [{"to": "m.step.G", "rel": "instance-of"}, {"to": "src.agent.dep", "rel": "cites"}, {"to": "wp.GL", "rel": "suggests"}], "source": "labyrinth"}, {"id": "th.G2.1", "title": "Theorem 1.1 and Corollary 7.1", "statement": "Every Barnette graph has a Hamiltonian cycle avoiding any prescribed edge.", "where": ["O Thm 1.1, Cor. 7.1; L Thm 3; T Thm 1.1"], "tags": ["step G2"], "created": "2026-11-14", "updated": "2026-11-14", "kind": "theorem", "tier": "T1", "status": "established (LIT: 2026 preprint or its formalisation, unrefereed)", "links": [{"to": "m.step.G2", "rel": "instance-of"}, {"to": "src.O", "rel": "cites"}, {"to": "src.L", "rel": "cites"}, {"to": "src.T", "rel": "cites"}, {"to": "wp.HC", "rel": "supports"}], "source": "labyrinth"}, {"id": "th.G2.2", "title": "Lean formalisation (OpenAI repository)", "statement": "31 Lean files, 8936 lines, importing only Mathlib. The formal statement is the conjecture as usually stated (lean/DIFFERENCES.md A). Corollary 7.2 and Theorem 2.2 are not formalised.", "where": ["research/2026-11-14_opus_history-literature/REPORT.md; research/2026-11-14_opus_lean-formalisation/graph/"], "tags": ["step G2"], "created": "2026-11-14", "updated": "2026-11-14", "kind": "theorem", "tier": "T1", "status": "established (LIT: published)", "links": [{"to": "m.step.G2", "rel": "instance-of"}, {"to": "src.agent.lean", "rel": "cites"}, {"to": "src.agent.hist", "rel": "cites"}, {"to": "wp.HC", "rel": "supports"}], "source": "labyrinth"}, {"id": "th.G2.3", "title": "consequences: P4 for Pfaffian braces", "statement": "The auditor could not open the cited theorem; the corollary is indeterminate in dep/audit-openai.md.", "where": ["O Cor. 7.2 via Gorsky-Steiner-Wiederrecht Thm 4.16"], "tags": ["step G2"], "created": "2026-11-14", "updated": "2026-11-14", "kind": "theorem", "tier": "T1", "status": "established (LIT: 2026 preprint or its formalisation, unrefereed)", "links": [{"to": "m.step.G2", "rel": "instance-of"}, {"to": "src.O", "rel": "cites"}, {"to": "wp.HC", "rel": "supports"}], "source": "labyrinth"}, {"id": "p.G2.4", "title": "all three proofs close", "statement": "Reverse-Lamport audits: O and T follow as written; L follows once four routine unwritten steps are supplied.", "where": ["research/2026-11-14_fable_dependency-graph/REPORT.md (verdicts)"], "tags": ["step G2"], "created": "2026-11-14", "updated": "2026-11-14", "kind": "theorem", "tier": "T2", "status": "established: proved here (unreviewed)", "review": {"state": "unreviewed", "date": "2026-11-14"}, "links": [{"to": "m.step.G2", "rel": "instance-of"}, {"to": "src.agent.dep", "rel": "cites"}, {"to": "wp.HC", "rel": "supports"}], "source": "labyrinth"}, {"id": "k.G2.5", "title": "ordered pairs of edges (global H+-)", "statement": "Some Hamiltonian cycle contains x and avoids y for any two edges. Not implied by the theorem.", "where": ["research/2026-11-14_opus_history-literature/REPORT.md (still open)"], "tags": ["step G2"], "created": "2026-11-14", "updated": "2026-11-14", "kind": "conjecture", "tier": "T5", "status": "open", "test": "lim/data/strengthenings.json searched every edge pair on the small corpus; extend the search or find a counterexample across a 3-edge cut.", "links": [{"to": "m.step.G2", "rel": "instance-of"}, {"to": "src.agent.hist", "rel": "cites"}, {"to": "wp.HC", "rel": "suggests"}], "source": "labyrinth"}, {"id": "p.G2.6", "title": "an even number of avoiding cycles", "statement": "Every cubic bipartite graph has an even number of Hamiltonian cycles avoiding each edge.", "where": ["research/2026-11-14_sol_route-algebra/PROOF.md 2 (from Thomason 1978, Cor. 1.5)"], "tags": ["step G2"], "created": "2026-11-14", "updated": "2026-11-14", "kind": "theorem", "tier": "T2", "status": "established: proved here (unreviewed)", "review": {"state": "unreviewed", "date": "2026-11-14"}, "links": [{"to": "m.step.G2", "rel": "instance-of"}, {"to": "src.agent.alg", "rel": "cites"}, {"to": "wp.HC", "rel": "supports"}], "source": "labyrinth"}, {"id": "th.S0.4", "title": "algebraic dual from grid minors (Lean)", "statement": "The formal proof never uses faces or the Jordan curve theorem. A drawn graph is a minor of a grid, the grid has an explicit algebraic dual, and duality survives deletion and contraction.", "where": ["research/2026-11-14_opus_lean-formalisation/DIFFERENCES.md items 1-6"], "tags": ["step S0"], "created": "2026-11-14", "updated": "2026-11-14", "kind": "theorem", "tier": "T1", "status": "established (LIT: 2026 preprint or its formalisation, unrefereed)", "links": [{"to": "m.step.S0", "rel": "instance-of"}, {"to": "src.Lean", "rel": "cites"}, {"to": "wp.T", "rel": "supports"}], "source": "labyrinth"}, {"id": "th.A.Lean", "title": "Hall's theorem on a gadget; global density bound (Lean)", "statement": "Max-flow is replaced by Hall's theorem on a doubled incidence graph, and the density bound is proved for all of T[U] at once from a rank inequality.", "where": ["research/2026-11-14_opus_lean-formalisation/DIFFERENCES.md items 9-11"], "tags": ["step A"], "created": "2026-11-14", "updated": "2026-11-14", "kind": "theorem", "tier": "T1", "status": "established (LIT: 2026 preprint or its formalisation, unrefereed)", "links": [{"to": "m.step.A", "rel": "instance-of"}, {"to": "src.Lean", "rel": "cites"}, {"to": "wp.P", "rel": "supports"}], "source": "labyrinth"}, {"id": "th.C.Lean", "title": "raw index sum, phase I^(n/3) (Lean)", "statement": "The formal proof never shows J is an integer. It keeps the sum of delta and needs only phase(n +- 6) = -phase(n).", "where": ["research/2026-11-14_opus_lean-formalisation/DIFFERENCES.md item 14"], "tags": ["step C"], "created": "2026-11-14", "updated": "2026-11-14", "kind": "theorem", "tier": "T1", "status": "established (LIT: 2026 preprint or its formalisation, unrefereed)", "links": [{"to": "m.step.C", "rel": "instance-of"}, {"to": "src.Lean", "rel": "cites"}, {"to": "wp.M", "rel": "supports"}], "source": "labyrinth"}, {"id": "th.N.Lean", "title": "polynomial prod(1 + aX), weights from one flow on G (Lean)", "statement": "Z is a polynomial, not an exponential sum, and Lemma 5.3 is replaced by a single flow on G with charge +1 at every white vertex. Proved for an abstract triangle system with two hypotheses on cycle index sums.", "where": ["research/2026-11-14_opus_lean-formalisation/DIFFERENCES.md items 15-18"], "tags": ["step N"], "created": "2026-11-14", "updated": "2026-11-14", "kind": "theorem", "tier": "T1", "status": "established (LIT: 2026 preprint or its formalisation, unrefereed)", "links": [{"to": "m.step.N", "rel": "instance-of"}, {"to": "src.Lean", "rel": "cites"}, {"to": "wp.N", "rel": "supports"}], "source": "labyrinth"}, {"id": "th.R.Lean", "title": "add a sink at the third root (Lean)", "statement": "Out-degree at most one and no directed cycle make a forest; no component count.", "where": ["research/2026-11-14_opus_lean-formalisation/DIFFERENCES.md item 20"], "tags": ["step R"], "created": "2026-11-14", "updated": "2026-11-14", "kind": "theorem", "tier": "T1", "status": "established (LIT: 2026 preprint or its formalisation, unrefereed)", "links": [{"to": "m.step.R", "rel": "instance-of"}, {"to": "src.Lean", "rel": "cites"}, {"to": "wp.IT", "rel": "supports"}], "source": "labyrinth"}, {"id": "th.E.Lean", "title": "the white tree is never built (Lean)", "statement": "The formal proof selects P inside G and skips the tree on the white faces.", "where": ["research/2026-11-14_opus_lean-formalisation/DIFFERENCES.md item 19"], "tags": ["step E"], "created": "2026-11-14", "updated": "2026-11-14", "kind": "theorem", "tier": "T1", "status": "established (LIT: 2026 preprint or its formalisation, unrefereed)", "links": [{"to": "m.step.E", "rel": "instance-of"}, {"to": "src.Lean", "rel": "cites"}, {"to": "wp.WT", "rel": "supports"}], "source": "labyrinth"}, {"id": "th.F.Lean", "title": "cut-balance lemma against the disk identity (Lean)", "statement": "A directed cycle of selected edges is a state cycle; a cut all of whose edges lie in a perfect matching contradicts |cut| = 2|black cut| + 3. No induced trees, no Jordan curve.", "where": ["research/2026-11-14_opus_lean-formalisation/DIFFERENCES.md items 21-22"], "tags": ["step F"], "created": "2026-11-14", "updated": "2026-11-14", "kind": "theorem", "tier": "T1", "status": "established (LIT: 2026 preprint or its formalisation, unrefereed)", "links": [{"to": "m.step.F", "rel": "instance-of"}, {"to": "src.Lean", "rel": "cites"}, {"to": "wp.JC", "rel": "supports"}], "source": "labyrinth"}, {"id": "th.G.Lean", "title": "glue perfect matchings across the 3-cut (Lean)", "statement": "Strong induction on the H- form: a perfect matching containing e with connected complement. Splitting is contraction.", "where": ["research/2026-11-14_opus_lean-formalisation/DIFFERENCES.md items 23-25"], "tags": ["step G"], "created": "2026-11-14", "updated": "2026-11-14", "kind": "theorem", "tier": "T1", "status": "established (LIT: 2026 preprint or its formalisation, unrefereed)", "links": [{"to": "m.step.G", "rel": "instance-of"}, {"to": "src.Lean", "rel": "cites"}, {"to": "wp.GL", "rel": "supports"}], "source": "labyrinth"}, {"id": "c.G2.c", "title": "the Lean proof builds here; standard axioms only", "statement": "#print axioms gives propext, Classical.choice, Quot.sound. No sorry, admit, axiom or native_decide in the 31 files.", "where": ["research/2026-11-14_opus_lean-formalisation/REPORT.md item 1; research/2026-11-14_opus_lean-formalisation/build/axioms.out (Lean 4.34.1, Mathlib d13f23b7)"], "tags": ["step G2"], "created": "2026-11-14", "updated": "2026-11-14", "kind": "evidence", "tier": "T4", "status": "computed", "evidence": ["research/2026-11-14_opus_lean-formalisation/REPORT.md item 1; research/2026-11-14_opus_lean-formalisation/build/axioms.out (Lean 4.34.1, Mathlib d13f23b7)"], "links": [{"to": "m.step.G2", "rel": "instance-of"}, {"to": "src.agent.lean", "rel": "cites"}, {"to": "wp.HC", "rel": "tests"}], "source": "labyrinth"}, {"id": "p.A.GT", "title": "one exact Euler identity, both directions", "statement": "m - b - (2p - 4) = (1/2) sum over non-face regions of (beta - (d - 4)). Every region contributes at most 0 except a black-inner separating triangle, which contributes +1.", "where": ["research/2026-11-14_opus_route-graph-theory/PROOFS.md Lemma 2.2, Thm 2.1"], "tags": ["step A"], "created": "2026-11-14", "updated": "2026-11-14", "kind": "theorem", "tier": "T2", "status": "established: proved here (under review)", "review": {"state": "under-review", "date": "2026-11-14", "by": ["research/2026-11-14_sol_referee-structure"], "verdict": "proof in research/2026-11-14_opus_route-graph-theory: pending (audit started 10:50 UTC)"}, "links": [{"to": "m.step.A", "rel": "instance-of"}, {"to": "src.agent.gt", "rel": "cites"}, {"to": "wp.P", "rel": "supports"}, {"to": "x.U.x5", "rel": "refutes"}, {"to": "th.A.T", "rel": "modifies"}], "source": "labyrinth"}, {"id": "p.A.6", "title": "pairs are alpha-orientations (Felsner lattice)", "statement": "Pairs up to reversing D-cycles are the alpha-orientations of H - t0 with out-degrees 2, d_x - 2, d_r - 1. They form a distributive lattice.", "where": ["research/2026-11-14_opus_route-graph-theory/PROOFS.md Lemma 3.1"], "tags": ["step A"], "created": "2026-11-14", "updated": "2026-11-14", "kind": "theorem", "tier": "T2", "status": "established: proved here (under review)", "review": {"state": "under-review", "date": "2026-11-14", "by": ["research/2026-11-14_sol_referee-structure"], "verdict": "proof in research/2026-11-14_opus_route-graph-theory: pending (audit started 10:50 UTC)"}, "links": [{"to": "m.step.A", "rel": "instance-of"}, {"to": "src.agent.gt", "rel": "cites"}, {"to": "wp.P", "rel": "supports"}], "source": "labyrinth"}, {"id": "h.A.5", "title": "Hall-free shelling construction", "statement": "Build a pair by a canonical ordering from t0 inward, as for Schnyder woods.", "where": ["research/2026-11-14_opus_route-graph-theory/ROUTES.md F2"], "tags": ["step A"], "created": "2026-11-14", "updated": "2026-11-14", "kind": "hunch", "tier": "T6", "status": "live", "vivid": "The certificate of non-existence is always one triangle, which is how density conditions behave when a shelling exists.", "test": "Implement a shelling of the 55 triangulations up to 14 vertices and compare with the flow pairs.", "links": [{"to": "m.step.A", "rel": "instance-of"}, {"to": "src.agent.gt", "rel": "cites"}, {"to": "wp.P", "rel": "suggests"}], "source": "labyrinth"}, {"id": "x.U.x5", "title": "LP relaxation of the three constraints is integral", "statement": "Perfect matchings of B intersected with complements of spanning trees of H form an integral polytope.", "where": ["research/2026-11-14_opus_route-graph-theory/ROUTES.md M1-M4; posed in research/2026-11-14_sol_machinery-literature/ROUTE-IDEAS.md 5"], "tags": ["step U"], "created": "2026-11-14", "updated": "2026-11-14", "kind": "deadend", "tier": null, "status": "refuted", "lesson": "Not matroid intersection. No constraint is redundant, tree matchings are not the bases of a matroid, and the abstract problem (a perfect matching with spanning-tree complement in a planar bipartite graph of maximum degree 3) is NP-hard. Any min-max must use the hexagonal faces of spoke graphs.", "note": "Smallest known counterexample: 13 vertices: a fractional vertex, the average of two non-tree states.", "links": [{"to": "m.step.U", "rel": "instance-of"}, {"to": "src.agent.gt", "rel": "cites"}, {"to": "src.agent.mach", "rel": "cites"}], "source": "labyrinth"}, {"id": "x.U.x6", "title": "induction by degree-4 expansion", "statement": "Lift a tree matching through the expansion of a degree-4 vertex.", "where": ["research/2026-11-14_opus_route-graph-theory/ROUTES.md I1; research/2026-11-14_opus_route-graph-theory/PROOFS.md"], "tags": ["step U"], "created": "2026-11-14", "updated": "2026-11-14", "kind": "deadend", "tier": null, "status": "refuted", "lesson": "States lift bijectively, but the lift of a tree matching is a tree matching only under a side condition at the split vertex that is not inherited. Contraction can create the obstruction.", "note": "Smallest known counterexample: Contraction creates a black-inner triangle from 11 vertices on; not every tree matching lifts (708 of 1516 cases in gt/ROUTES.md I1).", "links": [{"to": "m.step.U", "rel": "instance-of"}, {"to": "src.agent.gt", "rel": "cites"}], "source": "labyrinth"}, {"id": "x.U.x7", "title": "positivity of the norm or of the Laplacian terms", "statement": "CT F(w)F(1/w) and the constrained Laplacian coefficient are sums of nonnegative terms, so they are positive.", "where": ["research/2026-11-14_fable_referee-determinants/REPORT.md; research/2026-11-14_sol_route-algebra/PROOF.md 6"], "tags": ["step U"], "created": "2026-11-14", "updated": "2026-11-14", "kind": "deadend", "tier": null, "status": "refuted", "lesson": "Both are positive exactly when F is not zero, and showing that is the paper's argument. The positivity in the proof sits on the D side, these on the s side.", "note": "Smallest known counterexample: Obstructed roots of the double octahedron (9 vertices): 360 restricted white trees, 16 with one spoke per face, no tree matching.", "links": [{"to": "m.step.U", "rel": "instance-of"}, {"to": "src.agent.refd", "rel": "cites"}, {"to": "src.agent.alg", "rel": "cites"}], "source": "labyrinth"}, {"id": "x.U.x8", "title": "tree count as an unrooted invariant", "statement": "The number of tree matchings equals a fixed evaluation of a Tutte, Penrose, Tait or flow invariant of G.", "where": ["research/2026-11-14_sol_route-algebra/REPORT.md; research/2026-11-14_sol_route-algebra/invariants.json"], "tags": ["step U"], "created": "2026-11-14", "updated": "2026-11-14", "kind": "deadend", "tier": null, "status": "refuted", "lesson": "The count depends on the root.", "note": "Smallest known counterexample: plantri graph 12_2: 2 or 8 tree matchings depending on the black root, all roots with a pair.", "links": [{"to": "m.step.U", "rel": "instance-of"}, {"to": "src.agent.alg", "rel": "cites"}], "source": "labyrinth"}, {"id": "x.N.x14", "title": "single-swap descent on the number of Q-cycles", "statement": "From every pair with a cycle in Q_s some single exchange lowers the number of cycles.", "where": ["research/2026-11-14_opus_route-graph-theory/ROUTES.md D1, D2"], "tags": ["step N"], "created": "2026-11-14", "updated": "2026-11-14", "kind": "deadend", "tier": null, "status": "refuted", "lesson": "A swap along a Hamiltonian D-cycle exchanges r and s, so it cannot lower the count both ways. Exhaustive data up to 15 vertices had made it look true.", "note": "Smallest known counterexample: 50 vertices, 4-connected (gt/code/data/deeptrap_50_1.json); two swaps suffice there.", "links": [{"to": "m.step.N", "rel": "instance-of"}, {"to": "src.agent.gt", "rel": "cites"}], "source": "labyrinth"}, {"id": "x.N.x15", "title": "a global sign gauge on incidences", "statement": "Flip signs of incidence variables so that all tree matchings get the same coefficient sign.", "where": ["research/2026-11-14_sol_route-algebra/PROOF.md 2; research/2026-11-14_sol_route-algebra/REPORT.md"], "tags": ["step N"], "created": "2026-11-14", "updated": "2026-11-14", "kind": "deadend", "tier": null, "status": "refuted", "lesson": "A GF(2) inconsistency certificate: six tree states in which every incidence appears an even number of times, three with negative sign.", "note": "Smallest known counterexample: A 13-vertex instance with six tree matchings.", "links": [{"to": "m.step.N", "rel": "instance-of"}, {"to": "src.agent.alg", "rel": "cites"}], "source": "labyrinth"}, {"id": "k.N.11", "title": "non-increasing exchange path to a tree pair", "statement": "From every pair there is a path of exchanges along which the number of Q-cycles never increases and reaches 0. A sign-free form of T Theorem 5.2.", "where": ["research/2026-11-14_opus_route-graph-theory/ROUTES.md D3"], "tags": ["step N"], "created": "2026-11-14", "updated": "2026-11-14", "kind": "conjecture", "tier": "T5", "status": "open", "test": "Search exchange graphs at 20 to 30 vertices for a pair with no non-increasing path; at 80 vertices look for pairs with no decrease within two swaps.", "links": [{"to": "m.step.N", "rel": "instance-of"}, {"to": "src.agent.gt", "rel": "cites"}, {"to": "wp.N", "rel": "suggests"}], "source": "labyrinth"}, {"id": "k.N.12", "title": "Li's marked objects are the e_m coefficient", "statement": "Li's objects with cmin distinct marks are the elementary symmetric reading of the linear-weight coefficient.", "where": ["research/2026-11-14_fable_referee-determinants/REPORT.md (still open)"], "tags": ["step N"], "created": "2026-11-14", "updated": "2026-11-14", "kind": "conjecture", "tier": "T5", "status": "open", "test": "Enumerate Li's objects on an instance with cmin = 2 and compare the signed count with the coefficient of x^2 in F(1 + xa) for unit marks.", "links": [{"to": "m.step.N", "rel": "instance-of"}, {"to": "src.agent.refd", "rel": "cites"}, {"to": "wp.N", "rel": "suggests"}], "source": "labyrinth"}, {"id": "p.C.5", "title": "parity is enough three times; the value 3 once", "statement": "In the real-sign form, reversing a Q_s-cycle or a D-cycle multiplies the sign by (-1)^length. So the cancellation, the forest lemma and the permutation sign need only that state cycles are odd. The value 3 is needed once, to align the signs of the minimal D groups.", "where": ["research/2026-11-14_fable_referee-determinants/SIGNIFICANCE.md"], "tags": ["step C"], "created": "2026-11-14", "updated": "2026-11-14", "kind": "theorem", "tier": "T2", "status": "established: proved here (unreviewed)", "review": {"state": "unreviewed", "date": "2026-11-14"}, "links": [{"to": "m.step.C", "rel": "instance-of"}, {"to": "src.agent.refd", "rel": "cites"}, {"to": "wp.M", "rel": "supports"}, {"to": "x.C.x1", "rel": "refutes"}], "source": "labyrinth"}, {"id": "k.D.7", "title": "extremes of Felsner's lattice of pairs", "statement": "The face-flip minimum or the face-flip maximum of the lattice of pairs always carries a tree pair. Each extreme alone fails (at 40 and at 80 vertices); they never failed together in 180 random instances.", "where": ["research/2026-11-14_opus_route-graph-theory/ROUTES.md O4-O6"], "tags": ["step D"], "created": "2026-11-14", "updated": "2026-11-14", "kind": "conjecture", "tier": "T5", "status": "open", "test": "Exhaustive census of Eulerian triangulations up to about 24 vertices, where one-cycle covers start to appear.", "links": [{"to": "m.step.D", "rel": "instance-of"}, {"to": "src.agent.gt", "rel": "cites"}, {"to": "wp.FP", "rel": "suggests"}], "source": "labyrinth"}, {"id": "h.D.8", "title": "finding a tree matching is in PPAD", "statement": "Each phase of the marked walk follows a path in a graph of degree at most 2 with alternating signs.", "where": ["research/2026-11-14_opus_route-graph-theory/REPORT.md (leads)"], "tags": ["step D"], "created": "2026-11-14", "updated": "2026-11-14", "kind": "hunch", "tier": "T6", "status": "live", "vivid": "Would place the search problem between polynomial time and the 3^(N/4) enumeration.", "test": "Write one phase of T Theorem 5.3 as an END-OF-LINE instance with polynomial-size circuits for the two involutions.", "links": [{"to": "m.step.D", "rel": "instance-of"}, {"to": "src.agent.gt", "rel": "cites"}, {"to": "wp.FP", "rel": "suggests"}], "source": "labyrinth"}, {"id": "p.G.5", "title": "sharp primal hypothesis; gluing at black-inner cuts", "statement": "In G the obstruction is a non-trivial 3-edge cut whose black-ended side avoids b0. Li's cyclic 4-edge-connectivity is sufficient and not sharp: 1714 of 4494 rooted instances with separating triangles still have pairs.", "where": ["research/2026-11-14_opus_route-graph-theory/PROOFS.md; research/2026-11-14_opus_route-graph-theory/ROUTES.md G"], "tags": ["step G"], "created": "2026-11-14", "updated": "2026-11-14", "kind": "theorem", "tier": "T2", "status": "established: proved here (under review)", "review": {"state": "under-review", "date": "2026-11-14", "by": ["research/2026-11-14_sol_referee-structure"], "verdict": "proof in research/2026-11-14_opus_route-graph-theory: pending (audit started 10:50 UTC)"}, "links": [{"to": "m.step.G", "rel": "instance-of"}, {"to": "src.agent.gt", "rel": "cites"}, {"to": "wp.GL", "rel": "supports"}], "source": "labyrinth"}, {"id": "p.B.6", "title": "Hopf's Umlaufsatz for a flat angle structure", "statement": "The state defines a flat signed-angle structure, and a vertex-local identity says turning equals minus colour winding. The disk identity is then the theorem of turning tangents. Orientation-free form: every chosen-edge disk has W = B + 1.", "where": ["research/2026-11-14_opus_route-topology/REPORT.md item 1; research/2026-11-14_opus_route-topology/ROUTES.md"], "tags": ["step B"], "created": "2026-11-14", "updated": "2026-11-14", "kind": "theorem", "tier": "T2", "status": "established: proved here (unreviewed)", "review": {"state": "unreviewed", "date": "2026-11-14"}, "links": [{"to": "m.step.B", "rel": "instance-of"}, {"to": "src.agent.topo", "rel": "cites"}, {"to": "wp.DI", "rel": "supports"}], "source": "labyrinth"}, {"id": "p.K.4", "title": "cancellation as a Morse matching", "statement": "Pairs are combinatorial vector fields, tree pairs are gradient fields, and the cancellation reverses a closed orbit. The matching is acyclic and its critical cells are the forest pairs. Its Euler characteristic Z(1) is always 0, so a graded version is needed.", "where": ["research/2026-11-14_opus_route-topology/REPORT.md item 2"], "tags": ["step K"], "created": "2026-11-14", "updated": "2026-11-14", "kind": "theorem", "tier": "T2", "status": "established: proved here (unreviewed)", "review": {"state": "unreviewed", "date": "2026-11-14"}, "links": [{"to": "m.step.K", "rel": "instance-of"}, {"to": "src.agent.topo", "rel": "cites"}, {"to": "wp.K", "rel": "supports"}], "source": "labyrinth"}, {"id": "p.F.6", "title": "Alexander duality: #cycles = 1 + b1(P)", "statement": "The complement of the 2-factor retracts onto P, so the number of cycles is one more than the first Betti number of P.", "where": ["research/2026-11-14_opus_route-topology/REPORT.md item 2"], "tags": ["step F"], "created": "2026-11-14", "updated": "2026-11-14", "kind": "theorem", "tier": "T2", "status": "established: proved here (unreviewed)", "review": {"state": "unreviewed", "date": "2026-11-14"}, "links": [{"to": "m.step.F", "rel": "instance-of"}, {"to": "src.agent.topo", "rel": "cites"}, {"to": "wp.JC", "rel": "supports"}], "source": "labyrinth"}, {"id": "p.U.7", "title": "sutured Floer support; point-rainbow white trees", "statement": "A tree matching exists iff a named spin-c structure is supported in the sutured Floer homology of the special alternating link of G (Juhasz-Kalman-Rasmussen), iff the white faces have a 'point-rainbow' spanning tree.", "where": ["research/2026-11-14_opus_route-topology/REPORT.md item 3 (conditional on T Thm 1.2); COMPUTED on 826 instances up to 16 vertices"], "tags": ["step U"], "created": "2026-11-14", "updated": "2026-11-14", "kind": "theorem", "tier": "T2", "status": "established: proved here (unreviewed)", "review": {"state": "unreviewed", "date": "2026-11-14"}, "links": [{"to": "m.step.U", "rel": "instance-of"}, {"to": "src.agent.topo", "rel": "cites"}, {"to": "wp.FP", "rel": "supports"}], "source": "labyrinth"}, {"id": "k.U.7h", "title": "local search from a point-rainbow tree", "statement": "Same-colour exchanges from a point-rainbow tree, never increasing the black excess, reach a tree matching. A proof would be a cancellation-free local-search proof of the key lemma.", "where": ["research/2026-11-14_opus_route-topology/REPORT.md item 4 (R6'); 450 of 450 instances up to 16 vertices"], "tags": ["step U"], "created": "2026-11-14", "updated": "2026-11-14", "kind": "conjecture", "tier": "T5", "status": "open", "test": "Measure plateaus and step counts at 18 to 20 vertices (1543 triangulations at 18); look for a potential such as black excess plus a white-tree distance.", "links": [{"to": "m.step.U", "rel": "instance-of"}, {"to": "src.agent.topo", "rel": "cites"}, {"to": "wp.FP", "rel": "suggests"}], "source": "labyrinth"}, {"id": "x.D.x1", "title": "the partner of a tree matching is a tree matching", "statement": "Swapping r and s would then be a free involution on tree matchings and explain why their number is even.", "where": ["research/2026-11-14_opus_route-topology/REPORT.md item 5"], "tags": ["step D"], "created": "2026-11-14", "updated": "2026-11-14", "kind": "deadend", "tier": null, "status": "refuted", "lesson": "The evenness is a signed cancellation, Z(1) = 0, and not a free involution. Forest pairs are not symmetric in r and s.", "note": "Smallest known counterexample: 6 failures among 442 tree matchings tested (topo data; smallest instance not recorded here).", "links": [{"to": "m.step.D", "rel": "instance-of"}, {"to": "src.agent.topo", "rel": "cites"}], "source": "labyrinth"}, {"id": "c.U.c", "title": "cycle-to-state map replayed; it is not injective", "statement": "Every Hamiltonian cycle avoiding e0* gives a state, and that state is a tree matching or has no partner. Two cycles can give the same unpartnered state: on the hexagonal prism 4 avoiding cycles give 3 states, on the rim-8 bipyramid 6 cycles give 4 states.", "where": ["research/2026-11-14_fable_labyrinth-map/spotcheck.py (bipyramids of rim 4, 6, 8, every edge of t0)"], "tags": ["step U"], "created": "2026-11-14", "updated": "2026-11-14", "kind": "evidence", "tier": "T4", "status": "computed", "evidence": ["research/2026-11-14_fable_labyrinth-map/spotcheck.py (bipyramids of rim 4, 6, 8, every edge of t0)"], "links": [{"to": "m.step.U", "rel": "instance-of"}, {"to": "src.agent.map", "rel": "cites"}, {"to": "wp.FP", "rel": "tests"}], "source": "labyrinth"}, {"id": "p.G.6", "title": "boundary states of a cut; complete at lengths 3 and 4", "statement": "Cut T along a cycle, record a finite boundary state on each side, glue: the number of Hamiltonian cycles is the linear term of a Temperley-Lieb pairing of two disk vectors. O Theorem 2.2 says every piece with triangular boundary realises all 3 boundary patterns; for quadrilaterals all 6 patterns allowed by a parity rule occur (conditional on O; one sub-case by a finite computation).", "where": ["research/2026-11-14_fable_route-categorical/PROOF.md Prop. 1, Theorem 3; research/2026-11-14_fable_route-categorical/CATEGORICAL.md"], "tags": ["step G"], "created": "2026-11-14", "updated": "2026-11-14", "kind": "theorem", "tier": "T2", "status": "established: proved here (unreviewed)", "review": {"state": "unreviewed", "date": "2026-11-14"}, "links": [{"to": "m.step.G", "rel": "instance-of"}, {"to": "src.agent.cat", "rel": "cites"}, {"to": "wp.GL", "rel": "supports"}], "source": "labyrinth"}, {"id": "x.G.x2", "title": "a uniform boundary condition for longer cuts", "statement": "Induct along cuts of length 5 or more with a boundary condition that every piece satisfies.", "where": ["research/2026-11-14_fable_route-categorical/PROOF.md Prop. 4; research/2026-11-14_fable_route-categorical/EXPERIMENTS.md"], "tags": ["step G"], "created": "2026-11-14", "updated": "2026-11-14", "kind": "deadend", "tier": null, "status": "refuted", "lesson": "The realised boundary patterns depend on the piece from length 5 on. The prescribed edge is the complete boundary datum of a triangular cut, and that is why the induction stops at separating triangles.", "note": "Smallest known counterexample: A piece with 9 vertices and a 5-cycle boundary (cat data); checked up to 13 vertices.", "links": [{"to": "m.step.G", "rel": "instance-of"}, {"to": "src.agent.cat", "rel": "cites"}], "source": "labyrinth"}, {"id": "x.A.x2", "title": "two roots instead of a root face", "statement": "Root at two vertices not tied to a face; every root pair then has a two-root tree matching, and it gives a Hamiltonian cycle.", "where": ["research/2026-11-14_fable_route-categorical/REPORT.md (what failed); research/2026-11-14_fable_route-categorical/CATEGORICAL.md"], "tags": ["step A"], "created": "2026-11-14", "updated": "2026-11-14", "kind": "deadend", "tier": null, "status": "refuted", "lesson": "Two roots move the obstruction and do not remove it, and O Lemma 6.2 needs all roots on one side of every selected cycle, which a root face guarantees. This is what the face root is for.", "note": "Smallest known counterexample: Existence: plantri graph 12_6, 51 of 66 root pairs. Recovery: a two-root tree matching with no Hamiltonian cycle at 8 vertices.", "links": [{"to": "m.step.A", "rel": "instance-of"}, {"to": "src.agent.cat", "rel": "cites"}], "source": "labyrinth"}, {"id": "x.K.x2", "title": "the two cube structures form a bicomplex", "statement": "Treat reversal of Q-cycles and reversal of D-cycles as two commuting differentials and read the proof as homology.", "where": ["research/2026-11-14_fable_route-categorical/REPORT.md (what failed)"], "tags": ["step K"], "created": "2026-11-14", "updated": "2026-11-14", "kind": "deadend", "tier": null, "status": "refuted", "lesson": "The two reversals do not commute. The homological packaging is a restatement and gives no new existence argument.", "note": "Smallest known counterexample: 12 of 624 pairs up to 12 vertices.", "links": [{"to": "m.step.K", "rel": "instance-of"}, {"to": "src.agent.cat", "rel": "cites"}], "source": "labyrinth"}, {"id": "x.U.x9", "title": "topological Hall for partial tree matchings", "statement": "Get a tree matching from connectivity of the complex of partial tree matchings.", "where": ["research/2026-11-14_fable_route-categorical/REPORT.md (what failed)"], "tags": ["step U"], "created": "2026-11-14", "updated": "2026-11-14", "kind": "deadend", "tier": null, "status": "refuted", "lesson": "The complex is not connected enough even in the smallest case.", "note": "Smallest known counterexample: Octahedron: a two-face subcomplex is disconnected.", "links": [{"to": "m.step.U", "rel": "instance-of"}, {"to": "src.agent.cat", "rel": "cites"}], "source": "labyrinth"}, {"id": "p.TG.7", "title": "acyclic triangle factor of a second trinity K", "statement": "Hamiltonian cycles with a root on each side are the acyclic triangle factors of a second trinity K built from T. A symmetric restatement of the goal.", "where": ["research/2026-11-14_fable_route-categorical/PROOF.md Prop. 5"], "tags": ["step TG"], "created": "2026-11-14", "updated": "2026-11-14", "kind": "theorem", "tier": "T2", "status": "established: proved here (unreviewed)", "review": {"state": "unreviewed", "date": "2026-11-14"}, "links": [{"to": "m.step.TG", "rel": "instance-of"}, {"to": "src.agent.cat", "rel": "cites"}, {"to": "wp.2T", "rel": "supports"}], "source": "labyrinth"}, {"id": "k.U.8h", "title": "every Hamiltonian cycle from a two-root tree matching", "statement": "With two roots allowed to vary, the state method sees every Hamiltonian cycle.", "where": ["research/2026-11-14_fable_route-categorical/REPORT.md (table); COMPUTED up to 12 vertices"], "tags": ["step U"], "created": "2026-11-14", "updated": "2026-11-14", "kind": "conjecture", "tier": "T5", "status": "open", "test": "Decide it at 13 to 16 vertices; if it holds, find the modified disk identity for cycles that separate the roots.", "links": [{"to": "m.step.U", "rel": "instance-of"}, {"to": "src.agent.cat", "rel": "cites"}, {"to": "wp.FP", "rel": "suggests"}], "source": "labyrinth"}, {"id": "k.D.9", "title": "contraction inequality tau(T) >= tau(T/ac)", "statement": "Contracting a degree-4 vertex never increases the number of tree matchings. An injection behind it would lift tree matchings along vertex splits.", "where": ["research/2026-11-14_fable_route-categorical/REPORT.md (table); 948 contractions"], "tags": ["step D"], "created": "2026-11-14", "updated": "2026-11-14", "kind": "conjecture", "tier": "T5", "status": "open", "test": "Look for a local rule extending a tree matching across a vertex split on the 948 cases (cat/code/recursion.py contract).", "links": [{"to": "m.step.D", "rel": "instance-of"}, {"to": "src.agent.cat", "rel": "cites"}, {"to": "wp.FP", "rel": "suggests"}], "source": "labyrinth"}, {"id": "e.census16", "kind": "exhaustive", "tier": "T3", "status": "exhaustive for |V(T)| <= 16: two independent implementations agree instance by instance (no referee yet)", "title": "Census of states, pairs and tree matchings, all rooted Eulerian triangulations with at most 16 vertices", "statement": "7868 rooted triangulations (T, t0), 5088 with a pair. The multiset of (states, pairs, tree matchings) per size is the same in the combinatorics lab (own generator) and in the algebra agent's counts (plantri 4.5 and workings/barnette_lab). Totals: 140358 states, 24744 pairs, 24090 tree matchings. Positive tree-matching counts seen: 2, 4, 6, 8, 10, 12, 14, 16. A third enumeration by the combinatorics referee agrees as well.", "evidence": ["labyrinth/make_frontier.py", "labyrinth/frontier_crosscheck.json", "research/2026-11-14_fable_route-combinatorics/data/rows_v16.json", "research/2026-11-14_sol_route-algebra/counts.json", "research/2026-11-14_astra_referee-combinatorics/code/"], "review": {"state": "unreviewed", "date": "2026-11-14"}, "links": [{"to": "wp.FP", "rel": "tests"}, {"to": "k.D.3", "rel": "supports"}, {"to": "src.agent.comb", "rel": "cites"}, {"to": "src.agent.alg", "rel": "cites"}, {"to": "src.agent.refc", "rel": "cites"}], "created": "2026-11-14", "updated": "2026-11-14", "tags": [], "source": "labyrinth"}, {"id": "f.octahedron", "kind": "family", "status": "named example", "title": "Octahedron (dual of the cube), 6 vertices", "statement": "n = 3, 2 states, 2 pairs, 2 tree matchings, cmin = 1. The smallest case; with zero weights Z = i - i = 0.", "links": [{"to": "wp.FP", "rel": "instance-of"}], "tier": null, "created": "2026-11-14", "updated": "2026-11-14", "tags": [], "source": "labyrinth"}, {"id": "f.prism", "kind": "family", "status": "named example", "title": "Hexagonal prism dual (hexagonal bipyramid), 8 vertices", "statement": "3 states, 2 pairs, 2 tree matchings. The smallest example that separates: acyclic Q_s from tree matching, the raw determinant from its squarefree coefficient, trinity arborescences from tree matchings, avoiding cycles (4) from recovered cycles (2).", "links": [{"to": "x.R.x1", "rel": "supports"}, {"to": "x.N.x9", "rel": "supports"}, {"to": "x.U.x3", "rel": "supports"}, {"to": "x.A.x1", "rel": "supports"}], "tier": null, "created": "2026-11-14", "updated": "2026-11-14", "tags": [], "source": "labyrinth"}, {"id": "f.double-octahedron", "kind": "family", "status": "named example", "title": "Octahedron glued into a face of an octahedron, 9 vertices", "statement": "The smallest separating triangle. With t0 on the black side: 4 states, 4 pairs, 4 tree matchings, cmin = 2. With t0 on the white side: 4 states, no pair.", "links": [{"to": "th.A.T", "rel": "instance-of"}, {"to": "x.N.x7", "rel": "supports"}], "tier": null, "created": "2026-11-14", "updated": "2026-11-14", "tags": [], "source": "labyrinth"}, {"id": "f.rarity", "kind": "family", "status": "named family", "title": "q prism duals glued into white faces, 5q + 3 vertices", "statement": "3^q states and 2^q tree matchings: tree matchings are exponentially rare among states.", "links": [{"to": "p.D.5", "rel": "instance-of"}], "tier": null, "created": "2026-11-14", "updated": "2026-11-14", "tags": [], "source": "labyrinth"}, {"id": "f.cls6-12", "kind": "family", "status": "named example", "title": "12 vertices, class 6, t0 = (2,7,3)", "statement": "9 states, 10 pairs, 8 tree matchings. Highest partnered state, all-counterclockwise minimal cover and largest rank difference all fail here; its mirror image gives the lowest-state and clockwise failures.", "links": [{"to": "x.N.x4", "rel": "supports"}, {"to": "x.N.x5", "rel": "supports"}, {"to": "x.N.x6", "rel": "supports"}], "tier": null, "created": "2026-11-14", "updated": "2026-11-14", "tags": [], "source": "labyrinth"}, {"id": "f.cls158-16", "kind": "family", "status": "named example", "title": "16 vertices, class 158, t0 = (2,7,3)", "statement": "A single 13-cycle cover whose two orientations both have a triangle in Q_s. The only failure of 'every cover has a forest orientation' among 5088 rooted cases.", "links": [{"to": "x.N.x3", "rel": "supports"}], "tier": null, "created": "2026-11-14", "updated": "2026-11-14", "tags": [], "source": "labyrinth"}, {"id": "f.split4", "kind": "family", "status": "named example", "title": "Octahedron with every face split in four, 18 vertices (n = 15)", "statement": "20 pairs; the 4 one-cycle pairs all have a cycle in Q_s and the 12 tree pairs all have three cycles. The smallest example found with cyclic Q_s, three D-cycles and one exchange component.", "links": [{"to": "x.N.x3", "rel": "supports"}], "tier": null, "created": "2026-11-14", "updated": "2026-11-14", "tags": [], "source": "labyrinth"}, {"id": "f.theta26", "kind": "family", "status": "named example", "title": "Theta of three cubes-minus-an-edge, 26 vertices", "statement": "2-connected, cubic, bipartite, planar, non-Hamiltonian. 8 states and no pair at every root.", "links": [{"to": "x.S0.x2", "rel": "supports"}], "tier": null, "created": "2026-11-14", "updated": "2026-11-14", "tags": [], "source": "labyrinth"}, {"id": "f.torus3x3", "kind": "family", "status": "named example", "title": "3 x 3 triangular grid on the torus", "statement": "No two induced trees; the dual has 36 Hamiltonian cycles; a root-free pair exists with Q unicyclic.", "links": [{"to": "x.S0.x3", "rel": "supports"}], "tier": null, "created": "2026-11-14", "updated": "2026-11-14", "tags": [], "source": "labyrinth"}, {"id": "m.two-evaluations", "kind": "method", "status": "the one new idea", "title": "A signed sum over pairs, evaluated two ways", "statement": "Fix s and cancel (support on forest pairs); fix D and factor (nonzero at lowest order). Absent from the Barnette literature before 2026.", "links": [{"to": "wp.M", "rel": "uses"}, {"to": "wp.FP", "rel": "supports"}], "tier": null, "created": "2026-11-14", "updated": "2026-11-14", "tags": [], "source": "labyrinth"}, {"id": "m.enumeration", "kind": "method", "status": "tool", "title": "Exhaustive enumeration of rooted Eulerian triangulations", "statement": "Three independent generators (comb/lab/tri.py; plantri 4.5 in workings/barnette_lab; gt/code/tri.py) agree on the class counts 1, 0, 1, 1, 2, 2, 8, 8, 32, 57, 185 for 6 to 16 vertices.", "links": [{"to": "e.census16", "rel": "supports"}], "tier": null, "created": "2026-11-14", "updated": "2026-11-14", "tags": [], "source": "labyrinth"}, {"id": "m.reverse-lamport", "kind": "method", "status": "tool", "title": "Reverse-Lamport audit", "statement": "Conclusion-first AND/OR obligation graphs; used on the three papers and on the weight-free proof.", "links": [{"to": "p.G2.4", "rel": "supports"}], "tier": null, "created": "2026-11-14", "updated": "2026-11-14", "tags": [], "source": "labyrinth"}, {"id": "q.poly-time", "kind": "question", "tier": null, "status": "open", "title": "Is there a polynomial-time construction of the Hamiltonian cycle?", "statement": "The proofs give exponential search (3^(N/4), or 2^O(n log n) for the marked walk). Sampling states is polynomial; tree matchings can be exponentially rare.", "why": "The proofs give exponential search (3^(N/4), or 2^O(n log n) for the marked walk). Sampling states is polynomial; tree matchings can be exponentially rare.", "next": "First test: on the 40-vertex and 80-vertex failures of the lattice extremes (gt/code/data), check conjecture k.D.7 (min or max carries a tree pair) and count the orientations to be searched.", "links": [{"to": "k.D.7", "rel": "suggests"}, {"to": "h.D.8", "rel": "suggests"}, {"to": "h.U.2h", "rel": "suggests"}, {"to": "h.N.3", "rel": "suggests"}], "created": "2026-11-14", "updated": "2026-11-14", "tags": [], "source": "labyrinth"}, {"id": "q.structural", "kind": "question", "tier": null, "status": "open", "title": "Is there an existence proof with no signed count?", "statement": "Every reformulation of the target is exact and none has an existence argument; every structural shortcut tested fails on long D-cycles.", "why": "Every reformulation of the target is exact and none has an existence argument; every structural shortcut tested fails on long D-cycles.", "next": "First test: conjecture k.U.7h (local search from a point-rainbow tree, 450 of 450 so far) at 18 vertices, recording the longest plateau; then k.N.11 on exhaustive exchange graphs at 20 to 30 vertices.", "links": [{"to": "k.U.7h", "rel": "suggests"}, {"to": "k.N.11", "rel": "suggests"}, {"to": "h.U.1h", "rel": "suggests"}, {"to": "h.N.5", "rel": "suggests"}], "created": "2026-11-14", "updated": "2026-11-14", "tags": [], "source": "labyrinth"}, {"id": "q.canonical", "kind": "question", "tier": null, "status": "open", "title": "Is there a canonical forest pair?", "statement": "Seven extremal rules are refuted. Only 'lattice min or max' survives, on random samples.", "why": "Seven extremal rules are refuted. Only 'lattice min or max' survives, on random samples.", "next": "First test: exhaustive census to 24 vertices for k.D.7; apply the Hine-Kalman bijection to the 8 tree matchings of f.cls6-12 (k.D.6).", "links": [{"to": "k.D.7", "rel": "suggests"}, {"to": "k.D.6", "rel": "suggests"}], "created": "2026-11-14", "updated": "2026-11-14", "tags": [], "source": "labyrinth"}, {"id": "q.tm-lower", "kind": "question", "tier": null, "status": "open", "title": "How many tree matchings must there be?", "statement": "Proved here: at least 2 cmin. Conjectured: at least 2^cmin (no exception among 5088 cases; equality in 4609).", "why": "Proved here: at least 2 cmin. Conjectured: at least 2^cmin (no exception among 5088 cases; equality in 4609).", "next": "First test: extend the census to 17 and 18 vertices restricted to cmin >= 3 and look for a count below 8.", "links": [{"to": "k.D.3", "rel": "suggests"}, {"to": "p.D.2", "rel": "suggests"}], "created": "2026-11-14", "updated": "2026-11-14", "tags": [], "source": "labyrinth"}, {"id": "q.det", "kind": "question", "tier": null, "status": "open", "title": "Is there a determinant or Pfaffian for Z without coefficient extraction?", "statement": "Z is the squarefree coefficient of one determinant (2^n evaluations). Raw determinants, doubled edges and scalar factorisations are refuted.", "why": "Z is the squarefree coefficient of one determinant (2^n evaluations). Raw determinants, doubled edges and scalar factorisations are refuted.", "next": "First test: Grassmann-Pluecker identities for the six-value turn signature (h.N.4); the hexagonal prism polynomial is the regression test.", "links": [{"to":